                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_uncertainty:   `<strong>Correct — this is genuine uncertainty.</strong> The shape of the AI-audio shift is unknowable on your timetable. Naming it as uncertainty rather than risk is what frees you to act-to-learn instead of demanding a business case that cannot honestly exist.`,
                    coach_r1_risk:          `<strong>That's false precision.</strong> You're about to put a probability on the software market — but where would that number come from? This isn't a dice roll with known odds; it's genuine uncertainty. A false probability gives you false confidence. Decide as if you admit you don't know the odds.`,
                    coach_r1_nothing:       `<strong>“Nothing to decide yet” is paralysis in disguise.</strong> Flat sell-through is exactly what genuine uncertainty looks like before it resolves — and it resolves the same week the option closes. Waiting is a decision, and it's spending your runway.`,
                    coach_r1_reframe_good:  `<strong>A generative reframe.</strong> You've turned the threat into an opportunity framing you can actually test — trusted hardware in an open ecosystem, or a move into the service layer. Now the question is which assumption makes it real.`,
                    coach_r1_defend:        `<strong>Defence is a frame, not the only one.</strong> Containing the threat keeps you reactive and cedes the service layer by default. Reframing means generating several plausible readings — at least one of them an opportunity.`,
                    coach_r1_ignore:        `<strong>Calling it a niche is a bet, too.</strong> Keeping the multi-year cadence unchanged assumes the category isn't being redefined — the one thing you can't yet know. You've forfeited option value to avoid a decision.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp.</strong> You named genuine uncertainty, a generative reframe, AND the make-or-break assumption — willingness-to-pay. That assumption is testable cheaply, which is exactly what makes the next round possible.`,
                    coach_r1_noassumption:  `<strong>“Obviously right” is the most dangerous phrase in innovation.</strong> A reframe with no killable assumption can't be tested — so it can't be wrong, and it can't be trusted. Name the one belief a cheap probe could destroy.`,
                    coach_r1_lowcandor:     `<strong>Low candor is how you fall in love with a reframe.</strong> If you won't hold the framing as a hypothesis, you'll read every probe as confirmation. Reframing earns its keep only if you're willing to be proven wrong.`,
                    coach_r2_efficient:     `<strong>That's buying information cheaply.</strong> A spread of cheap, sharp probes — including a direct willingness-to-pay signal — moves your key belief for a fraction of the pivot's cost, and you can walk away from every one.`,
                    coach_r2_portfolio:     `<strong>A reasonable portfolio.</strong> You're learning without betting. Make sure at least one probe attacks the willingness-to-pay question head-on — that's the assumption that decides everything.`,
                    coach_r2_single:        `<strong>One probe is a start, not a portfolio.</strong> A single experiment leaves you exposed to its idiosyncrasies. Two or three cheap, complementary probes triangulate the belief far better per euro.`,
                    coach_r2_expensive:     `<strong>The acqui-hire teaches you a lot — but at a price.</strong> €1.5M for one signal is heavy when a fake-door test and a retailer trial would move the same belief for a fraction. Buy the cheap learning first.`,
                    coach_r2_sunkcost:      `<strong>That six-month build is a sunk-cost trap.</strong> €1.2M and half a year in, if it goes badly will you actually shelve it — or will you have spent so much you commit anyway? A probe you can't abandon isn't a probe; it's a premature commitment with extra steps.`,
                    coach_r2_confirm:       `<strong>A probe designed to confirm can't surprise you.</strong> If you've engineered the experiment to validate the reframe you love, you've spent money to feel certain, not to learn. Design it so a bad result would actually change your mind.`,
                    coach_r3_honest:        `<strong>That's honest updating.</strong> Holding the engagement AND the price signal at once is hard — most teams celebrate one and bury the other. You've measured what the probes actually moved, not what you hoped they would.`,
                    coach_r3_cherrypick:    `<strong>You're hearing only the half you like.</strong> The pilot showed high engagement and almost no willingness to pay — and you're celebrating the engagement. You reframed beautifully and now you can't hear evidence against it. What would a skeptic on your team say this means?`,
                    coach_r3_abandon:       `<strong>Don't over-correct.</strong> One weak price signal isn't fatal — strong engagement is real option value. Abandoning the whole reframe on a single negative reading throws away what the probes bought you.`,
                    coach_r3_invent:        `<strong>Where did 65% come from?</strong> You've converted genuine uncertainty into a precise-looking probability and you're about to bank on it. That false precision is exactly the trap — the number feels like knowledge but it's a guess in a lab coat.`,
                    coach_r3_staged:        `<strong>A staged option set is the frontier move.</strong> You've narrowed honestly and kept the right to abort. That's how you act under uncertainty without betting the firm on a still-foggy thesis.`,
                    coach_r3_commitnow:     `<strong>That's premature commitment.</strong> The evidence is mixed and you're recommending the full €40M pivot on it. You skipped the part where you confirm anyone will pay. What did you commit before you needed to?`,
                    coach_r3_probeagain_ok: `<strong>One more sharp probe is fine — for now.</strong> Targeting the willingness-to-pay gap is the right question. But the learning curve flattens; know in advance what result would finally let you commit.`,
                    coach_r3_probeagain_late: `<strong>This is where probing becomes paralysis.</strong> The runway is nearly gone and you're queuing another experiment. At some point the cost of another probe exceeds its value — and that point is now.`,
                    coach_r3_shelve:        `<strong>Shelving forfeits the option.</strong> You did the work to reduce the uncertainty and then declined to use it. The entrant is signing your partners and hiring your engineers; the shelf is not a safe place.`,
                    coach_r3_overcertain:   `<strong>You're claiming near-certainty.</strong> Four probes don't dissolve genuine uncertainty to under 10% remaining — that's the false-precision reflex again. Calibrated confidence leaves room for the unknown you haven't seen.`,
                    coach_r3_undersold:     `<strong>You learned more than you're admitting.</strong> Saying almost nothing was resolved discounts real signals and pushes you toward endless probing. Give the evidence its due.`,
                    coach_r4_stagednow:     `<strong>That's frontier timing.</strong> A staged bet, sized to the evidence, committed now while the window is open and the option is still live — you've acted when enough uncertainty was resolved, not when certainty arrived.`,
                    coach_r4_fullnow_early: `<strong>Too big, too soon.</strong> You're committing the full €40M while the fog is still thick. The board asked what evidence you have that anyone will pay — and the honest answer is “not enough yet.” A staged bet would have bought the next decision.`,
                    coach_r4_fullnow_ok:    `<strong>A bold full commitment.</strong> With this much uncertainty resolved it's defensible — but a staged release with triggers would have preserved the right to abort if the next signal turned.`,
                    coach_r4_wait:          `<strong>You waited until the option closed.</strong> Two more quarters of data arrive the same week the entrant signs your last partner and hires your engineers. The data you wanted won't make the decision easier — it will make it irrelevant.`,
                    coach_r4_walkaway:      `<strong>You handed back the option.</strong> Protecting the core is defensible only if the uncertainty was truly unfavourable — but you reduced it and then declined to use it. The service layer is now ceded to the entrant.`,
                    coach_r4_oversized:     `<strong>The size doesn't fit the evidence.</strong> Going all-in before the willingness-to-pay question is settled is the premature-commitment error wearing a confident face. Stage the capital to the milestones.`,
                    coach_r4_notrigger:     `<strong>A bet with no trigger is a hope.</strong> Your staging is right, but “we'll know it when we see it” gives you nothing to accelerate or abort against. Define the milestone and the rival-deadline that move the decision.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
