                    insight_frontier:       `You embraced the unknown: you named genuine uncertainty, bought information cheaply, updated honestly, and committed a staged, triggered bet when enough fog had cleared and before the window shut. That is exactly the upside-of-uncertainty discipline — commitment as a deliberate frontier decision, not a wait for a certainty that never arrives.`,
                    insight_early:          `You bet too early: a full €40M commitment on a still-foggy thesis. The board's question — what evidence do you have that anyone will pay? — had no good answer because you skipped the cheap experiments that would have produced one.`,
                    insight_waited:         `You waited for certainty that genuine uncertainty cannot provide. By the time the data arrived the option had closed — partners signed, engineers gone, runway spent. Waiting felt safe and was the most expensive choice on the board.`,
                    insight_falseprecision: `You treated uncertainty as measurable risk — inventing probabilities and reading probes to confirm them. The numbers felt like knowledge but were guesses in a lab coat, and the false confidence they bought is the classic trap Furr warns against.`,
                    insight_partial:        `A partial read of the frontier: some good moves, but the reframe, the probing, the honest update and the timing didn't yet line up into a coherent bet. The discipline is to chain them — reframe, probe, update, commit.`,
                    insight_sunkcost:       `Your six-month build became a sunk cost: too expensive to abandon, so it pulled you toward commitment regardless of what it showed. A real probe is cheap enough to walk away from and sharp enough to surprise you.`,
                    insight_optionlost:     `Your option value collapsed — through defence, shelving, over-spending or simply waiting. Optionality has an expiry; the right to commit later is only valuable while it's still live.`,
                    insight_stillfoggy:     `You commit-or-decline with most of the fog intact. Cheap, sharp probes — especially on willingness-to-pay — would have moved the decisive belief far more before you had to choose.`,
                    insight_runway:         `You acted with runway and war chest to spare — the mark of buying information cheaply. You resolved enough uncertainty to commit without exhausting the resources that gave you the option in the first place.`,

                    hint_falseprecision:    `You're forcing odds onto genuine uncertainty. Drop the invented probabilities and decide on the strength of what the probes actually moved.`,
                    hint_sunkcost:          `A probe you can't abandon will force your hand. Favour cheap, time-boxed experiments you could shelve tomorrow.`,
                    hint_option:            `Your option value is collapsing. Stop forfeiting the right to commit later — defend it with staged, reversible moves.`,
